{
  "display": "form",
  "name": "processDataForm",
  "title": "Process data overview",
  "type": "form",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "content",
      "key": "uitleg",
      "input": false,
      "tableView": false,
      "html": "<h3>Process data</h3><p>This form is only about the variables a BPMN process holds. Two objects carry them: <code>zaak.zaakdata</code>, the process variables, and <code>taak.taakdata</code>, this task's stored data with the process variables merged in when it was opened. Neither is zaak data or taak data &mdash; for those, see the other two forms.</p><p>The first panel dumps every key each object holds. The second answers the question that follows from it: several process variables are copies of something the zaak already has, and the zaak is the one to read — for coverage, not because the copy is out of date. The third writes a variable, so you can watch one arrive. A misspelled property renders as empty here and is reported in the browser console instead, naming the full path it could not find.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "panel",
      "key": "de_variabelen",
      "title": "The process variables",
      "label": "Variabelen",
      "input": false,
      "tableView": false,
      "collapsible": false,
      "components": [
        {
          "type": "content",
          "key": "variabelen_uitleg",
          "input": false,
          "tableView": false,
          "html": "<h4>Every variable the process holds</h4><p>Two stages of the same data. <code>taak.taakdata</code> is what this task has stored &mdash; the process variables it was opened with, plus anything the form has saved. <code>zaak.zaakdata</code> is the process variables themselves, which only change when a task is completed. Save the field in the third panel and the difference between the two becomes visible.</p><p>Neither can be listed here, because the variables differ per process definition and per run. Address a variable you know by name: <code>zaak.zaakdata.zaakIdentificatie</code>. A variable holding an object or a list needs its own expression to look inside, such as <code>ZAC_opmaakLijst(zaak.zaakdata.documenten, &quot;titel&quot;)</code>.</p>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "panel",
      "key": "zaakdata_vs_zaak",
      "title": "Which one should a form read?",
      "label": "Vergelijking",
      "input": false,
      "tableView": false,
      "collapsible": false,
      "components": [
        {
          "type": "content",
          "key": "vergelijking_uitleg",
          "input": false,
          "tableView": false,
          "html": "<h4>Two values, one answer</h4><p>Each row is one value that exists in more than one place. <strong>Read the right-hand column.</strong> Not because the left one is out of date &mdash; ZAC does keep it in step: reassigning the zaak calls <code>changeZaakDataAssignment</code>, and changing the communicatiekanaal calls <code>updateCommunicationChannelZaakVariabele</code>. The reasons are different, and they matter more:</p><ul><li><strong>Coverage.</strong> These eight are all you get. The zaak has startdatum, status, resultaat, omschrijving, toelichting, deadlines, kenmerken, besluiten, related zaken &mdash; none of which is a process variable and none of which ever will be. Reading the zaak means never asking for a new variable to be added.</li><li><strong>The sync is a promise, not a mechanism.</strong> It holds because two specific call sites remember to update it. Any new route that changes a behandelaar has to remember too. The zaak cannot fall out of step with itself.</li><li><strong>Two of them never arrive at all.</strong> <code>zaakUUID</code> and <code>zaaktypeUUID</code> are stripped from the task data by <code>TaskRestService.readFilteredZaakdata</code>, which is why their left cell is empty while the zaak has the value.</li></ul><p>Where a row shows <em>also <code>taak.…</code></em>, the taak carries the same value and reads it from those same process variables, so it is a third copy of the same thing rather than a third option.</p><p>The one row that is not really a comparison: <code>zaakGroep</code> is the group handling the <em>zaak</em>, while <code>taak.groep</code> is the group this <em>task</em> is offered to. Usually equal, not the same thing.</p>"
        },
        {
